# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, git stores whatever the committing machine produced. Five
# files in this repository ended up with CRLF that way, and the symptom was a
# whole-file diff every time one of them was touched from a different
# environment — AUDIT-RESPONSE.md once reported 490 changed lines for no change
# at all.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository *and* checks out LF
# everywhere, including Windows. Modern editors handle LF without complaint,
# and a single endings convention is the only way the churn actually stops.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF wherever they are checked out, not merely in the
# repository.
#
# `.githooks/pre-push` was stored as CRLF, which made its first line
# `#!/bin/sh\r`. Linux and macOS read that as a request for an interpreter at
# the path `/bin/sh\r`, which does not exist, and refuse to run the hook with
# "bad interpreter". Git Bash on Windows tolerates it, so the failure is
# invisible to whoever wrote it — the hook simply stops running for everybody
# else, without ever saying so.
*.sh   text eol=lf
.githooks/*  text eol=lf

# Binary. Never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.docx   binary
*.pptx   binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.zip    binary
